TL;DR. A vLLM user reported that Qwen3-Omni crashes on a video with no audio track when use_audio_in_video=True1. My first fix silently fell back to use_audio_in_video=False. A maintainer&rsquo;s review asked for a clear ValueError instead, and a maintainer&rsquo;s cleanup commit then put the same check in the shared parent class. There it failed an existing cache-correctness test in CI, on two paths: the parent never sees the audio, and a cache hit arrives with empty input. The merged change touches 3 files2.
Why this matters. If you subclass a multimodal processor and add a check in _call_hf_processor, the same two paths apply to you. Both only showed up in CI.
What I believed The PR body argues that when no audio is extracted there is &ldquo;nothing to interleave&rdquo;, so passing the flag on only turns a usable result into a crash3. I did not see the downside myself. The reviewer raised it: a silent fallback hands the caller output that is not audio-in-video output, with no sign of that4.
What I did I came to this by scanning multimodal code for attributes that go missing on edge cases. An AI assistant traced the cause: the Hugging Face Qwen3OmniMoeProcessor calls next(audio_lengths) on an exhausted iterator and raises a bare StopIteration at line 2475. I confirmed the trace by reproducing it with a synthetic no-audio video of the reported shape (16, 3, 480, 256)1. vLLM wraps that in a generic ValueError, which is what the reporter saw.
After review I replaced the fallback with an explicit error in the Qwen3-Omni class6. A maintainer&rsquo;s cleanup commit then added a check to the shared Qwen2.5-Omni parent class as well7. That is where CI failed.
What happened A fresh-environment rerun on 2026-10-31, run by the AI assistant that helped draft this post, used transformers 5.22.1. use_audio_in_video=True raised the same StopIteration, now at line 2498. The same call with use_audio_in_video=False returned normal outputs. The line differs from the one in the PR, which does not state its transformers version; the behavior is the same.
Figure: why the guard raised on real audio and on cache hits. Redrawn from the author&rsquo;s PR comment of 2026-07-18, not a measurement. Generated by figure.py.
CI then failed, for real errors and not flakes. I investigated the failing jobs, reproduced them locally on a CPU build, and found two false positives:
The parent never sees the audio. The Qwen3-Omni processor pops mm_data[&quot;audios&quot;], builds mm_data[&quot;audio&quot;], and calls the Qwen2.5-Omni parent. The parent pops &quot;audios&quot; again, finds nothing, and raised even when real audio was present. A cache hit has no input. On a full multimodal-processor-cache hit, _call_hf_processor receives an empty mm_data. Both guards read that as &ldquo;no audio track&rdquo;. A third problem was already there: an assert &quot;audio&quot; in mm_counts fired whenever the audio limit was zero, whether or not the video lacked audio. It became the same clear error.
An AI wrote the second fix, which I reviewed and tested. The final guards require unconsumed video data and no &quot;audio&quot; key already present. I reported 6 tests passing locally on a CPU build across both models9. That count is from the PR thread, and I did not rerun vLLM&rsquo;s tests for this post.
Why it failed The first approach failed on review, not on a test. In my opinion the reviewer was right: a request for audio-in-video that quietly becomes video-only gives the caller a wrong result with no signal, and an error is cheaper to act on than a wrong answer.
The two guard failures showed up in CI. My explanation, labeled as such: one method is entered in three different ways, directly, through super() from a subclass that has already consumed part of its input, and on a cache hit that has no input at all. A check written for the first way is wrong for the other two. I did not test whether other processors in the same family share the parent and have the same two paths.
What I&rsquo;d do instead This is opinion, and untested beyond this one fix.
Test every way a method is entered. For a method that subclasses override and that a cache can bypass, write a case for the direct call, the call through super(), and the empty-input cache hit. Here the existing cache-correctness test caught both failures, which is the argument for keeping such tests in CI. Keep an edge-case input set for the whole model family. A video with no audio, a silent audio track, and an empty input, run against every processor that shares the base class. I have not built this. Make consumption explicit. When a subclass removes a key before delegating, say so in the signature or a short assertion at the boundary. A lint rule that flags the same key popped twice in an override chain is possible; I have not written one. Limits of this result What I didn&rsquo;t test: other processors, any transformers version but the one in the repro log8, and vLLM&rsquo;s test suite itself. Could this work elsewhere? I did not test any other setting. Open question: which other processors share the Qwen2.5-Omni parent, and do they have the same two paths? Reproduce uv venv --python 3.12 .venv uv pip install --python .venv/bin/python transformers==5.22.1 numpy torch torchvision .venv/bin/python repro.py repro.py and its log are in the draft folder. The log records versions and the line where StopIteration is raised.
References [1] vLLM issue 48383: https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/48383 [2] vLLM PR 48420: https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/48420
